  "worked_solution": "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1},$ $\\text{L2},$ and $\\text{L3}.$ From $\\text{L2},$ we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c.\n$$\n\nUsing $\\text{L1}$, we substitute ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ into this equation to obtain\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L3}.$ Thus, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{substituting }{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}\\text{ into }x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L7}.$ Using line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,c.\n$$\n\nFrom line $\\text{L6}$, we know that $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}).$ Substituting this expression for $c$ into the formula for $x(t)$ gives\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L7}.$ Thus, line $\\text{L7}$ can be obtained by $\\text{substituting }c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\text{ into }x(t)={e}^{At}c.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L8}$ and $\\text{L9}.$ Starting from $\\text{L8},$\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwe rewrite the exponent using the algebraic identity $At-A{t}_{0}=A(t-{t}_{0})$ to get\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L9}.$ Thus, $\\text{L9}$ follows from $\\text{L8}$ by the fact that $At-A{t}_{0}=A(t-{t}_{0})\\text{ for any }t\\text{ and }{t}_{0}.$",
